@page "/HowItWorks/reporting-funds"
@using InvestmentTaxCalculator.Components

<HowItWorksGuideLayout PageAddress="HowItWorks/reporting-funds">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="section">
            <p>
                Offshore reporting funds, such as many ETFs domiciled in Ireland or Luxembourg, report their income each
                year. Income the fund kept rather than paid out is "excess reportable income" (ERI). It is taxed as if it was
                paid to you, and it is added to the cost of your holding, so it is not taxed again when you sell. Brokers do
                not report ERI, so you add it on the <a href="AddExcessReportableIncome" class="link-styled">ERI and
                Equalisation</a> page. Run the calculation first, so that the page knows your holdings.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Adding excess reportable income</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    You enter the fund's reporting period end date, the ticker, the ERI per share from the fund's report, and
                    whether it is dividend or interest income.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The app takes your holding at the end of the reporting period, works out the total, and records it on the
                    distribution date, which it takes as six months after the period end (SI 2009/3001 reg. 94).
                </li>
                <li>
                    The income appears as Excess Reportable Income (Dividend) or (Interest) in your income for the tax year of
                    the distribution date.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The same amount is added to the cost of your holding. If you sold some units between the period end and
                    the distribution date, those units' share is added to the allowable cost of that sale (reg. 99). The rest
                    is added to the Section 104 pool on the distribution date.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Equalisation</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Part of a fund's first distribution after you buy can be equalisation, which is a return of part of what
                    you paid rather than income.
                </li>
                <li>
                    On the same page you pick the fund and the dividend or ERI entry it belongs to, and enter the equalisation
                    amount. The app reduces that income by the amount and reduces the cost of your holding by the same amount.
                </li>
                <li>
                    For an accumulating fund, add the ERI first and then attach the equalisation to that ERI entry.
                    Equalisation on ERI is split between the pool and sales after the period end in the same way as the ERI.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <div class="alert alert-info alert-custom" role="note">
                There is a special rule for reporting funds that are sold before the end of a reporting period and bought
                back within 30 days after it: you are treated as holding the units at the period end. The app does not
                apply this rule, so you need to make the adjustment yourself.
            </div>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <FurtherReading>
        <li><a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2009/3001/regulation/94" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Offshore Funds (Tax) Regulations 2009, reg. 94</a>: reported income</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2009/3001/regulation/99" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Offshore Funds (Tax) Regulations 2009, reg. 99</a>: disposals of interests</li>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/section-104-pool" class="link-styled">Section 104 pool</a></li>
    </FurtherReading>
</HowItWorksGuideLayout>
